From ad6921abb20869db7e6785a5a10141cefc5a1e0a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 14:43:47 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: limit the present blit to the sub-rect that actually
 changed

Real 486DX2-50 data from patch 0012's FLIPDBG.LOG found the actual
dominant per-frame cost, after three prior hypotheses (video flush
~50ms, render loop ~70ms, audio pump ~29ms) each came back real but not
dominant: SDL_BlitSurface() in flipScreen() -- the renderSurface ->
window-surface format-conversion blit -- measured ~145-160ms/frame, by
far the largest single cost found in this whole investigation. Combined
with the other three now-measured costs (~150ms total) and this new
number, the running total (~300ms) finally accounts for essentially all
of the real ~330ms average frame time.

Root cause: the blit was unconditional and full-surface
(SDL_BlitSurface(renderSurface, NULL, inScreen, NULL)), format-converting
all 640x480 = 307,200 pixels every frame regardless of how much of the
image actually changed. But blowupOntoScreen() (see blowUp.cpp, patch
0012) only ever writes a centered sub-rect of renderSurface -- on this
port's current 100x12 game image at 6x blowup, that's roughly 600x102 =
61,200 pixels, about 1/5th of the full surface. The rest is static
letterbox border that's black and unchanged frame to frame, except right
after a blowUpChanged clear (SDL_FillSurfaceRect over the whole surface,
which can change the border's visible size).

Fix: compute the same sub-rect blowupOntoScreen uses (blowUpFactor *
width/totalImageHeight, centered) and blit only that rect on a normal
frame; fall back to the original full-surface blit only on the frame
where blowUpChanged is still true (the one frame per change where the
letterbox border itself needs to be pushed to the screen too, since it
was just cleared). Uses two separate SDL_Rect values for src/dst rather
than sharing one -- SDL_BlitSurface's dstrect parameter is mutated
(clipped) in place, so aliasing it with the still-being-read srcrect
would be a real bug, not just untidy.

Verified under DOSBox-X: builds; visually confirmed clean rendering
across two blowup-factor changes (pressed B twice, screenshotted each
transition) -- no corruption, no stale border pixels left over from the
smaller/larger previous rect, letterbox stays correctly black on both
sides. FLIPDBG.LOG confirms the mechanism: blit_avg_ms dropped sharply
on this host too (many windows reading ~0ms now, vs. ~8.5-15.5ms
before this patch). Clean boot/play/ESCAPE-quit, no hang. Real-hardware
confirmation on the 486DX2-50 (both the fps number and a repeat
FLIPDBG.LOG capture) is the next step -- this is the first patch in
this investigation with a real chance of closing most of the gap to the
15fps KPI, not just ruling another hypothesis out.
---
 gameSource/game.cpp | 33 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/gameSource/game.cpp b/gameSource/game.cpp
index 53fd251..d7503d7 100644
--- a/gameSource/game.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/game.cpp
@@ -278,7 +278,38 @@ void flipScreen( SDL_Surface *inScreen ) {
     double blitStartTime = Time::getCurrentTime();
 #endif
 
-    SDL_BlitSurface( renderSurface, NULL, inScreen, NULL );
+    // DOS-PORT: blowupOntoScreen only ever writes a centered sub-rect of
+    // renderSurface (the actual blown-up game image) -- the rest of a
+    // full-screen (640x480) renderSurface is static letterbox border,
+    // unchanged frame to frame except right after a blowUpChanged clear.
+    // The original unconditional SDL_BlitSurface( ..., NULL, ..., NULL )
+    // format-converts all 307,200 pixels every frame regardless of how
+    // little of that actually changed. Real-hardware measurement
+    // (486DX2-50, FLIPDBG.LOG) found this costing ~145-160ms/frame -- by
+    // far the single largest per-frame cost found in this whole
+    // investigation, more than the render loop, the flush, and the audio
+    // pump combined. Limiting the blit to the sub-rect that actually
+    // changed cuts the pixel count roughly 5x on this port's current
+    // 100x12/6x-blowup configuration. Use two separate SDL_Rect values
+    // (not one shared between src/dst) since SDL_BlitSurface's dstrect
+    // parameter is mutated (clipped) in place -- aliasing it with srcrect
+    // would corrupt the still-unread source rect.
+    SDL_Rect srcRect;
+    srcRect.w = blowUpFactor * width;
+    srcRect.h = blowUpFactor * totalImageHeight;
+    srcRect.x = ( renderSurface->w - srcRect.w ) / 2;
+    srcRect.y = ( renderSurface->h - srcRect.h ) / 2;
+    SDL_Rect dstRect = srcRect;
+
+    if( blowUpChanged ) {
+        // the whole surface was just cleared this frame (letterbox
+        // border may have changed size too) -- blit everything, just
+        // this once.
+        SDL_BlitSurface( renderSurface, NULL, inScreen, NULL );
+        }
+    else {
+        SDL_BlitSurface( renderSurface, &srcRect, inScreen, &dstRect );
+        }
 
 #ifdef __DJGPP__
     double blitElapsed = Time::getCurrentTime() - blitStartTime;
-- 
